Z||000005||theogre||11-16-2000||07:56 PM||||I beleive we've established that the Fiero Store upgrade is the Grand Am upgrade in kit form.<P>If you shop the scrap yards you can get GA calipers for a dirt price (I paid $15 for a pair of nice ones) and rebuild them if need be for a couple bucks each. (rebuilding front calipers is easy and cheap.) Then you don't have to worry about core charge either.<P>Two of the best rotors at Advance, which were raybestos or bendix, are less than the price of one Fiero rotor. (about 60$ the pair Vs 70+ for the single fiero rotor.) You can get cheaper ones than that even if you go off brand. (I wouldn't waste time with a used rotor. Turning them cost 15-20$ each. may as well get new ones for the few dollars dif.)<P>The machine work has been running about $50 dollars according to various other threads.<P>you're looking at maybe $150 total and no rocket science to do it. (Costs a bit more if you get the calipers at the parts store.) Unless you have the extra cash to burn or are really not up to doing it I just can't see the price of the kit.<P>Tip: when taking used calipers cut the brake line and leave the cut piece attached to the caliper. stuff a nail into the line to prevent brake fluid from getting on stuff durring transit and storage.||||reg||1
